  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1205 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nSummer heat will return to the Mid-South with little in the way\nof organized rainfall over the next week.\n\nThe cutoff low over Georgia is beginning to dissipate and lift\nslowly north. Upper ridging nosing in from the Southern Plains,\ncombined with weak surface high pressure, will produce mostly\nbenign weather across the Mid-South today. There is a low chance\n(<10%) of a shower or thunderstorm near the Alabama/Mississippi\nborder or along the Tennessee River. Temperatures will climb a\ncouple of degrees warmer this afternoon with highs mainly in the\nupper 80s to lower 90s.\n\nTemperatures will climb into the lower to mid 90s on Thursday and\nlikely reach those levels each day well into next week.\nMeanwhile, humidity levels will slowly increase over the next\ncouple of days as light southerly winds develop and dewpoints\nclimb back into the 70s. The Delta region southwest of Memphis\nwill threaten to hit Heat Advisory criteria as early as Friday,\nwith much of the Mid-South potentially reaching criteria by early\nnext week. School is back, but summer is not over. Remember your\nheat safety guidelines like staying hydrated, taking breaks when\nworking outside, and checking the back seat.\n\nA broad upper trough moving through the Great Lakes and Ohio\nValley will extend south into the Mid-South and bring a low\nchance (20-30%) for showers and thunderstorms Friday night into\nSaturday.  After this weak trough moves through, and the upper\nridging re-establishes itself by Sunday, expect a summertime\npattern of isolated afternoon and evening showers and\nthunderstorms each day next week with hot and humid conditions.",
